{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"regular sequence regular sequence","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md","fileName":"Regular Sequence.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Regular Sequence","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 20:15\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regular Sequences and Syzygies]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Sequences and Syzygies","Depth of a Module","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"regular sequence regular sequence","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md","fileName":"Regular Sequence.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Regular Sequence","heading":"Regular Sequence","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A sequence r₁,…,rₙ is regular on M when r₁ is M-regular, each later rᵢ is regular on M/(r₁,…,rᵢ₋₁)M, and the final quotient is nonzero.\n\nInside the Jacobson radical, permutations and positive powers preserve regularity under the source's Noetherian finite-generation hypotheses. The maximum possible length in an ideal defines [[Depth of a Module]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Sequences and Syzygies","Depth of a Module","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"regular sequence regular sequence","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Sequence.md","fileName":"Regular Sequence.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Regular Sequence","heading":"References","sect